    "post_url": "https://www.tiktok.com/@bpthedev/photo/7519274254039305490",
    "posted": "2025-06-23",
    "caption": "My unbeatable stack for web developers 🔥\nWhen it comes to building web apps, this is the stack I swear by 👇 Next.js My absolute favourite tech in the world. It’s fast, flexible, and insanely productive. You can build entire apps just with Next alone. I still prefer the Pages Router for now, but the App Router has really levelled up recently. Tailwind CSS I’ve never liked writing CSS. Tailwind changed everything for me. It's the quickest way to build clean, beautiful UIs. No fussing with class names or nesting — just building. FluxUI To make Tailwind even better, we built our own internal UI package: FluxUI. Think Chakra UI vibes but with Tailwind under the hood. It gives our components structure while keeping the styling light and flexible. Prisma This is how I manage my database layer. With Prisma, defining models is simple, migrations are easy, and querying is actually enjoyable — plus it all works seamlessly with TypeScript. PostgreSQL The rock of our stack. Postgres is stable, powerful, and perfect for anything CRUD-based — whether it’s user accounts, payments, or complex data queries. I trust it for everything. Better Auth For auth, I use Better Auth. It’s open source and ridiculously easy to set up. It plays nicely with Prisma, supports all the major OAuth providers, and works across popular frameworks. Bonus: it’s beautifully documented. tRPC This is one of the most underrated tools in dev right now. No need to duplicate types or write complex API schemas. I define my backend logic once and get full end-to-end type safety in my frontend. It just works — fast, clean, and type-safe. Stripe The gold standard for payments. Whether I’m doing subscriptions, marketplaces, or one-off charges, Stripe makes it all easy. The dev experience is unreal. Their docs, SDKs, and test tools make integration painless. Nx Finally, for managing the repo itself — Nx is my go-to. It lets me manage everything from one place, share node_modules, and generate code from the CLI or UI. It’s an absolute game changer for scaling projects efficiently. If you’re building apps and want a modern, productive dev stack — this is what’s working for me every day. Let me know if you want a deeper dive into how I’ve set it all up 👇",
